    {
      "SurveyID": "SV_3C4qiwbWK4gkg06",
      "Element": "SQ",
      "PrimaryAttribute": "QID59",
      "SecondaryAttribute": "Q33-bge-m3__bge-reranker-v2-m3-chunk_b38bb9b365869b5e28b16150",
      "TertiaryAttribute": null,
      "Payload": {
        "QuestionText": "<p><strong>EDOS Label</strong></p><p><strong>2.1 descriptive attacks</strong> – Negative descriptive generalizations about women or gendered groups.</p><br><p><strong>Paragraph</strong></p><div style=\"white-space: pre-wrap\">Section: 2. Conceptualising and defining cyber violence against women and girls > 2.1. State of the art <sup>(1)</sup> > Trolling\n\nWhile cyber bullies are likely to have an existing relationship with victims, perpetrators of trolling are usually anonymous (CSES, 2019).\n\nTrolling ‘may function to establish an aggressive online area, rejecting new posters and discouraging the advancement of online communities’ (Bratu, 2017).\n\nGendertrolling is a term used to refer to gender-based insults, vicious language and rape and death threats by a coordinated group of trolls to humiliate women, particularly those who assert their opinion online (Mantilla, 2013).</div><br>",
        "DefaultChoices": false,
        "DataExportTag": "Q33-bge-m3__bge-reranker-v2-m3-chunk_b38bb9b365869b5e28b16150",
        "QuestionID": "QID59",
