    {
      "SurveyID": "SV_3C4qiwbWK4gkg06",
      "Element": "SQ",
      "PrimaryAttribute": "QID63",
      "SecondaryAttribute": "Q37-all-chunk_8eff3ec4f0b38dd6e80f2a48",
      "TertiaryAttribute": null,
      "Payload": {
        "QuestionText": "<p><strong>EDOS Label</strong></p><p><strong>2.2 aggressive and emotive attacks</strong> – Aggressive, degrading, or emotionally hostile insults against women, feminists, or gendered subjects.</p><br><p><strong>Paragraph</strong></p><div style=\"white-space: pre-wrap\">Section: 2. Conceptualising and defining cyber violence against women and girls > Flaming\n\nFlaming is a form of ‘aggressive, hostile, profanity-laced’ (O’Sullivan and Flanagin, 2003) online communication, which is always characterised by ‘insults, negative affect and “typographic energy” such as capital letters and exclamation marks’ (Jane, 2015). It entails deliberately ‘swearing or using otherwise offensive language’ (Moor, Heuvelman and Verleur, 2010) to express emotionally charged or contrarian statements, usually to elicit a response from another online user (CSES, 2019).</div><br>",
        "DefaultChoices": false,
        "DataExportTag": "Q37-all-chunk_8eff3ec4f0b38dd6e80f2a48",
        "QuestionID": "QID63",
        "QuestionType": "Slider",
        "Selector": "NumberScale",
