    {
      "SurveyID": "SV_3C4qiwbWK4gkg06",
      "Element": "SQ",
      "PrimaryAttribute": "QID66",
      "SecondaryAttribute": "Q44-qwen3-embedding-8b__qwen3-reranker-8b-chunk_1a9fc296cf26ccd722debde3",
      "TertiaryAttribute": null,
      "Payload": {
        "QuestionText": "<p><strong>EDOS Label</strong></p><p><strong>2.2 aggressive and emotive attacks</strong> – Aggressive, degrading, or emotionally hostile insults against women, feminists, or gendered subjects.</p><br><p><strong>Paragraph</strong></p><div style=\"white-space: pre-wrap\">Section: 2. SOCIETAL CONTEXT AND ROOT CAUSES OF CYBER VIOLENCE AND HATE SPEECH ONLINE AGAINST WOMEN > 3.1. The victims > 3.1.3. Women’s visibility and representation online\n\n<sup>76</sup> Amnesty International (2018), Op. Cit\n\n<sup>77</sup> Inter Parliamentary Union, “Sexism, harassment and violence against women parliamentarians”, Issues Brief, Oct. 2016, available at http://archive.ipu.org/pdf/publications/issuesbrief-e.pdf\n\n31\n\nPolicy Department for Citizens' Rights and Constitutional Affairs\n\nFemale politicians are overall targets for mob attacks and extremely violent cyber abuse. In the IPU study cited above, a Member of the European Parliament (MEP) explains that she “receive(s) emails, sometimes accompanied by pornographic images, and the message ‘get out of politics; get married instead.” Another Parliamentarian explains that she once received in four days more than 500 rape threats on Twitter. In the UK, ‘vile’ twitter attacks are broadly perceived as pushing women out of politics<sup>78</sup>.</div><br>",
        "DefaultChoices": false,
        "DataExportTag": "Q44-qwen3-embedding-8b__qwen3-reranker-8b-chunk_1a9fc296cf26ccd722debde3",
        "QuestionID": "QID66",
