    {
      "SurveyID": "SV_3C4qiwbWK4gkg06",
      "Element": "SQ",
      "PrimaryAttribute": "QID70",
      "SecondaryAttribute": "Q50-bge-m3__qwen3-reranker-8b-chunk_aba414b46b481c560c438282",
      "TertiaryAttribute": null,
      "Payload": {
        "QuestionText": "<p><strong>EDOS Label</strong></p><p><strong>2.3 dehumanising attacks &amp; overt sexual objectification</strong> – Dehumanization, animalization, sexual objectification, or reduction of women to sexual or body objects.</p><br><p><strong>Paragraph</strong></p><div style=\"white-space: pre-wrap\">Section: 2. Conceptualising and defining cyber violence against women and girls > 2.1. State of the art <sup>(1)</sup> > Cyber harassment / cyber bullying\n\nCyber harassment is a persistent and repeated course of conduct targeted at a specific person, designed to cause severe emotional distress and often a fear of physical harm (Council of Europe Cybercrime Convention Committee, 2018).\n\nIn cyber bullying, the focus is placed almost exclusively on the experiences of children, adolescents and young adults, characterised by legal and emotional vulnerability (Patchin, 2015; Wang et al., 2019).\n\nOnline hate speech targeting women usually involves sexualisation, objectification and body-shaming comments, as well as degrading comments and rape threats, often from members of incel communities (Santos, Amaral and Simões, 2020).\n\nSignificantly, the EU Code of Conduct’s definition of hate speech online does not mention gender, sexism or misogyny as it largely focuses on racism and xenophobia (European Commission, 2016).\n2. Conceptualising and defining cyber violence against women and girls</div><br>",
        "DefaultChoices": false,
        "DataExportTag": "Q50-bge-m3__qwen3-reranker-8b-chunk_aba414b46b481c560c438282",
        "QuestionID": "QID70",
        "QuestionType": "Slider",
        "Selector": "NumberScale",
