    {
      "SurveyID": "SV_3C4qiwbWK4gkg06",
      "Element": "SQ",
      "PrimaryAttribute": "QID77",
      "SecondaryAttribute": "Q57-bge-m3__bge-reranker-v2-m3-chunk_23932360ec17266e6cdaf717",
      "TertiaryAttribute": null,
      "Payload": {
        "QuestionText": "<p><strong>EDOS Label</strong></p><p><strong>2.3 dehumanising attacks &amp; overt sexual objectification</strong> – Dehumanization, animalization, sexual objectification, or reduction of women to sexual or body objects.</p><br><p><strong>Paragraph</strong></p><div style=\"white-space: pre-wrap\">Section: Services provided by women’s centres > Introduction\n\nSexualised violence is one of the most pervasive and harmful forms of violence against women and girls (VAWG), causing deep and lasting trauma to women and girls across Europe and beyond. It encompasses a continuum of behaviours that violate physical, psychological, and sexual integrity. It includes rape and sexual assault, coerced or unwanted sexual acts, sexual harassment, sexual exploitation, forced or non-consensual sexual touching, non-consensual image sharing, online sexual abuse, stalking with sexual intent, grooming, and child sexual abuse. It can occur in intimate relationships, within families, in workplaces and institutions, in public spaces, online, and in situations of conflict, displacement, or detention.</div><br>",
        "DefaultChoices": false,
        "DataExportTag": "Q57-bge-m3__bge-reranker-v2-m3-chunk_23932360ec17266e6cdaf717",
        "QuestionID": "QID77",
        "QuestionType": "Slider",
        "Selector": "NumberScale",
        "DataVisibility": {
          "Private": false,
          "Hidden": false
        },
        "Configuration": {
