    {
      "SurveyID": "SV_3C4qiwbWK4gkg06",
      "Element": "SQ",
      "PrimaryAttribute": "QID96",
      "SecondaryAttribute": "Q76-qwen3-embedding-8b__qwen3-reranker-8b-chunk_510fc2ba7254ba96b2aa40d1",
      "TertiaryAttribute": null,
      "Payload": {
        "QuestionText": "<p><strong>EDOS Label</strong></p><p><strong>3.2 immutable gender differences and gender stereotypes</strong> – Claims presenting gender differences or stereotypes as natural, fixed, biological, or inevitable.</p><br><p><strong>Paragraph</strong></p><div style=\"white-space: pre-wrap\">Section: Illustrative Gender Stereotypes and Gender Discriminatory Roles > media content > Using a Safety versus a Freedom Lens in the UK\n\nWhen messages were used instead to refer to women’s freedom and how VAWG may restrict women’s mobility, respondents reported being more alerted to the equality implications of VAWG and to the fact that the current situation is unfair and that it is not inevitable and does not have to be this way.\n\nSource: EVAW (2014) Unpublished public attitudes research among 1,000 UK adults by YouGov for the End Violence Against Women Coalition, UK.\n\nTo find the right “frame”, it is important to:</div><br>",
        "DefaultChoices": false,
        "DataExportTag": "Q76-qwen3-embedding-8b__qwen3-reranker-8b-chunk_510fc2ba7254ba96b2aa40d1",
