    {
      "SurveyID": "SV_3C4qiwbWK4gkg06",
      "Element": "SQ",
      "PrimaryAttribute": "QID99",
      "SecondaryAttribute": "Q79-qwen3-embedding-8b__bge-reranker-v2-m3-chunk_18b886f8590c50910598fe58",
      "TertiaryAttribute": null,
      "Payload": {
        "QuestionText": "<p><strong>EDOS Label</strong></p><p><strong>3.2 immutable gender differences and gender stereotypes</strong> – Claims presenting gender differences or stereotypes as natural, fixed, biological, or inevitable.</p><br><p><strong>Paragraph</strong></p><div style=\"white-space: pre-wrap\">Section: KEY TERMS AND CONCEPTS\n\nGender stereotypes: generalized views or preconceptions about attributes or characteristics that are or ought to be possessed by, or the roles that are or should be performed by, women and men (OHCHR).\n\nIntersectionality: a concept that seeks to capture both the structural and dynamic consequences of the interaction between two or more forms of discrimination or systems of subordination. It specifically addresses the manner in which racism, patriarchy, economic disadvantages and other discriminatory systems contribute to axes of inequality that structure the relative positions of women and men, races and other groups. Moreover, it addresses the way that specific acts and policies create burdens that flow along these intersecting axes actively contributing to create a dynamic of disempowerment (UN, 2000).</div><br>",
        "DefaultChoices": false,
        "DataExportTag": "Q79-qwen3-embedding-8b__bge-reranker-v2-m3-chunk_18b886f8590c50910598fe58",
        "QuestionID": "QID99",
