    {
      "SurveyID": "SV_3C4qiwbWK4gkg06",
      "Element": "SQ",
      "PrimaryAttribute": "QID101",
      "SecondaryAttribute": "Q81-bge-m3__bge-reranker-v2-m3-chunk_1cc058dfe70b48e3309c5eec",
      "TertiaryAttribute": null,
      "Payload": {
        "QuestionText": "<p><strong>EDOS Label</strong></p><p><strong>3.2 immutable gender differences and gender stereotypes</strong> – Claims presenting gender differences or stereotypes as natural, fixed, biological, or inevitable.</p><br><p><strong>Paragraph</strong></p><div style=\"white-space: pre-wrap\">Section: Illustrative Gender Stereotypes and Gender Discriminatory Roles\n\nCompounded gender stereotypes can have a disproportionate negative impact on certain groups of women, such as women in custody, women from minority or indigenous groups, women with disabilities, women from lower caste groups or with lower economic status, migrant women, LBTI women, etc. Changing social norms so that VAWG can be eliminated is inevitably a discussion about power between women and men. It is also a wider discussion about power between different social groups. Addressing VAWG requires rooting the discussion more broadly to encompass gender norms and the intersection of multiple inequalities, to ensure that the various risk factors that perpetuate discrimination and abuse are captured for all segments of the population.</div><br>",
        "DefaultChoices": false,
        "DataExportTag": "Q81-bge-m3__bge-reranker-v2-m3-chunk_1cc058dfe70b48e3309c5eec",
        "QuestionID": "QID101",
        "QuestionType": "Slider",
