    {
      "SurveyID": "SV_3C4qiwbWK4gkg06",
      "Element": "SQ",
      "PrimaryAttribute": "QID102",
      "SecondaryAttribute": "Q82-bge-m3__qwen3-reranker-8b-chunk_e76bd79d89d31f6cbdaf1022",
      "TertiaryAttribute": null,
      "Payload": {
        "QuestionText": "<p><strong>EDOS Label</strong></p><p><strong>3.2 immutable gender differences and gender stereotypes</strong> – Claims presenting gender differences or stereotypes as natural, fixed, biological, or inevitable.</p><br><p><strong>Paragraph</strong></p><div style=\"white-space: pre-wrap\">Section: 2. SOCIETAL CONTEXT AND ROOT CAUSES OF CYBER VIOLENCE AND HATE SPEECH ONLINE AGAINST WOMEN > KEY FINDINGS\n\nCyber violence and hate speech online against women are part of the continuum of violence against women. Normalisation of violence against women in the media leads to victim-blaming and the invisibilisation of victim’s perspective.\n Gender inequality in the tech sector reverberates on platforms and algorithms may reinforce gender biases.\n Anonymity, crowd culture and the permanence of harmful data online leads to multiple re-victimisations.\n\nFollowing the examination of definitions and types of violence in the previous chapter, it is now possible to look at the root causes of cyber violence and hate speech online against women. This chapter analyses how societal gender stereotypes, gender imbalances in the tech industry and the specific architecture of digital platforms create, drive and sustain the phenomenon.</div><br>",
        "DefaultChoices": false,
        "DataExportTag": "Q82-bge-m3__qwen3-reranker-8b-chunk_e76bd79d89d31f6cbdaf1022",
        "QuestionID": "QID102",
        "QuestionType": "Slider",
        "Selector": "NumberScale",
        "DataVisibility": {
          "Private": false,
          "Hidden": false
        },
