    {
      "SurveyID": "SV_3C4qiwbWK4gkg06",
      "Element": "SQ",
      "PrimaryAttribute": "QID103",
      "SecondaryAttribute": "Q83-qwen3-embedding-8b__bge-reranker-v2-m3-chunk_3f116839d0ce9137f4753399",
      "TertiaryAttribute": null,
      "Payload": {
        "QuestionText": "<p><strong>EDOS Label</strong></p><p><strong>3.2 immutable gender differences and gender stereotypes</strong> – Claims presenting gender differences or stereotypes as natural, fixed, biological, or inevitable.</p><br><p><strong>Paragraph</strong></p><div style=\"white-space: pre-wrap\">Section: Illustrative Gender Stereotypes and Gender Discriminatory Roles\n\n60% say “I don’t see my community of friends, family and acquaintances represented accurately in most advertising”.\n\n64% think “Advertisers need to do more to eliminate traditional or old-fashioned roles of men and women in their ads”.\n\n84% “really like when ads include a positive message about making the world better”.\n\n75% of consumers say they “feel more positive toward companies that demonstrate in their advertising that men and women have the same capabilities and roles”.\n\nIn addition to broad-based gender stereotypes that exist, different contexts generate unique social norms and gender roles, which can vary even within the same geographic or socio-political setting for different groups of women and girls (Fergus 2012).</div><br>",
        "DefaultChoices": false,
        "DataExportTag": "Q83-qwen3-embedding-8b__bge-reranker-v2-m3-chunk_3f116839d0ce9137f4753399",
        "QuestionID": "QID103",
        "QuestionType": "Slider",
